<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Release policy: cleartext is permitted.

  The socket is not the security boundary. Device-to-daemon traffic runs inside
  a Noise session whose daemon static key is pinned from the pairing payload
  (ADR-010, ADR-031), so a ws:// candidate adds no confidentiality or integrity
  loss: an on-path observer sees opaque Noise packets, and an impostor cannot
  complete the handshake without the pinned key. The h4rn-connect relay
  candidate is still wss:// and the relay only forwards Noise packets.

  Android's <domain-config> can name exact hosts and IP literals, but not IP
  ranges, so a release build cannot describe "RFC 1918, CGNAT/Tailscale and
  .local/MagicDNS" there. Those are exactly the candidates a phone needs to
  reach a daemon on the LAN or a tailnet, and falling back to the relay because
  the OS vetoed the LAN socket breaks "relay and Tailscale at the same time".
  The base config therefore permits cleartext for every host; TLS verification
  for https/wss still uses the system trust anchors. See docs/mobile.md and
  docs/transport.md.

  The debug source set keeps its own permissive config; this file is the one
  release builds ship.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
